  "solid_lock_cov_SC": "0.82",
  "locked_paragraph": "The locked test studies, evaluated once after all modelling decisions had been frozen, gave a more pessimistic picture than development CV. For bio-oil yield (556 runs, 28 studies) the STRATA base learner reached RMSE = 18.3 wt% (R\u00b2 = -0.95; best learner k-nearest neighbours, 12.9 wt%; mean predictor 13.6 wt%); for solid yield (457 runs, 30 studies) the STRATA base learner reached RMSE = 18.5 wt% (R\u00b2 = 0.30; best learner MLP, 14.4 wt%; mean predictor 23.0 wt%). For bio-oil, only 4 of the 11 non-trivial learners had a lower pooled RMSE than the mean predictor, although 6 had a lower median per-study RMSE: pooled errors were dominated by a few studies outside the bulk of the training distribution \u2014 for XGBoost, a single solvothermal series of 90 runs contributed 54% of the squared error, and excluding it (post hoc) lowered the RMSE from 18.3 to 13.6 wt% (R\u00b2 = 0.00). Study-weighted coverage of row-calibrated 90% intervals was 0.60 (95% CI 0.47\u20130.73) for bio-oil and 0.46 (95% CI 0.34\u20130.58) for solids; study-calibrated intervals reached 0.89 (95% CI 0.81\u20130.97) and 0.82 (95% CI 0.70\u20130.92) (Table 6, Fig. {FIGREF:fig05_coverage}). Study calibration thus removed most, but for solids not all, of the under-coverage; with 28\u201330 locked studies the intervals are wide, and the solid-yield shortfall reflects a few studies with feedstocks (technical lignin, plastic\u2013biomass blends) that are rare in the training data.",
  "abstract_locked_sentence": "On 38 locked studies, study-calibrated intervals covered 0.89 (bio-oil) and 0.82 (solids) of runs, versus 0.60 and 0.46 for row-calibrated intervals, and no learner predicted bio-oil yields of new studies substantially better than the training mean.",
  "hl_cov": "Study-calibrated intervals raised coverage from 0.46\u20130.61 to 0.82\u20130.91",
  "cov_range_RC": "0.46\u20130.61",
  "cov_range_SC": "0.82\u20130.91",
  "generalisation_paragraph": "Predicting studies published after 2018 from earlier studies gave RMSE = 19.4 (bio-oil; higher than the study-grouped CV value of 15.8 wt%) and 16.5 wt% (solids; higher than 14.1 wt%), i.e. models trained on older literature transfer no better to newer studies than to randomly chosen unseen studies. Leaving out one feedstock family at a time gave mean RMSE of 17.2 and 16.3 wt%, with the largest errors for the 'Other' category (bio-oil, 32.6 wt%) and 'Other' (solids, 29.8 wt%). On the independent 4TU hydrochar database (502 runs after removing 36 potential duplicates; 69 feedstock series), a solid-yield model trained on all development studies reached RMSE = 13.3 wt% (R\u00b2 = 0.31), whereas training on the 16 HTC development studies alone gave 16.7 wt% (R\u00b2 = -0.08), showing that the small HTC subset of the primary database does not by itself support transfer to other HTC compilations (Table 7).",
  "robustness_paragraph": "Adding Gaussian noise of 20% of the training standard deviation to continuous test features increased the bio-oil RMSE from 15.7 to 16.6 wt% and the solid RMSE from 13.9 to 15.4 wt%, while SC coverage was 0.90 without and 0.90 with noise (bio-oil) \u2014 the error budget for unseen studies is dominated by the study offset, not by input noise. Training on 25% instead of all training studies raised the bio-oil RMSE from 15.7 to 20.3 wt%, and SC coverage remained 0.89, whereas RC coverage stayed at 0.45\u20130.56 (Supplementary Fig. S1).",
  "efficiency_paragraph": "All learners trained in at most 9 s on one CPU thread (MLP); the STRATA base learner took 0.2 s and predicted 1,000 runs in 4 ms (Supplementary Table S17, Supplementary Fig. S2). The complete STRATA procedure \u2014 two inner five-fold cross-validations, quantiles, variance components and k-shot quantiles for all k \u2014 took 2.3\u20137.0 s on all development studies, against 0.3\u20130.5 s for fitting the base learner alone (measured, 1 and 4 threads); this overhead is negligible compared with the cost of a single hydrothermal experiment.",
  "error_paragraph": "Out-of-study errors were largest for Other feedstocks among families (bio-oil RMSE 33.9 wt%, 6 studies), for the \u2264250 \u00b0C temperature band (21.1 wt%) and for HTL solvothermal runs (18.5 wt%) (Supplementary Table S9). The five worst-predicted bio-oil studies had per-study RMSE between 35.1 and 61.4 wt% and absolute mean offsets of 26.7\u201360.9 wt% (processes: HTL 3, HTC 1, HTL solvothermal 1). Across extraction-solvent groups with at least three studies, the bio-oil RMSE ranged from 10.8 (hydrocarbon) to 19.8 wt% (none named) and the mean residual from -9.5 to +9.7 wt% (Supplementary Fig. S3).",
  "adaptive_sentence": "For bio-oil, SC-N gave study-weighted coverage 0.91 (SC 0.91) with median width 55.6 vs 59.5 wt% and mean interval score 84.2 vs 77.3; for solids, coverage 0.92 vs 0.91, median width 53.3 vs 45.7 wt%, interval score 69.5 vs 65.2. The adaptive variant therefore did not improve interval efficiency \u2014 both widths and interval scores were worse \u2014 although coverage across process subtypes became more uniform (largest deviation from 0.90: 0.03 vs 0.07 for solids). This negative result is consistent with the main finding that the dominant error component is a study offset that input features cannot localise. Because this variant was added after inspecting pipeline-verification results, it is reported as exploratory and is not part of the main claims."
